Novelty.strongest_argument:
The refutation rests on the trivial-recombination prong, and this run found a closer published prior for the measurement design than any the proposal cites.

The already-published prong fails again for the exact object. My 12 queries, wave 1's 137 and the repair's 20 found no paper that scores learned byte-level boundaries (H-Net stage-2 router scores) against alignment-consistent split pairs across translations.

The central measurement, however, is published, and the proposal does not cite it: scoring a segmenter's boundaries against the alignment-consistent split points of a sentence pair. Yarmohammadi, Rangarajan Sridhar, Bangalore and Sankaran 2013 (IJCNLP, ACL Anthology I13-1141; full text read):
- Section 2 defines "monotonic phrase alignments": segmentations of a sentence pair into phrase pairs consistent with the word alignment, in monotone order.
- Section 3.2 extracts every such segmentation from GIZA++ alignment matrices by dynamic programming.
- Eq. 4 scores a monolingual boundary classifier against those alignment-derived boundaries with precision, recall and F1 (P 70.51%, R 91.52%), using as gold the valid segmentation closest to the output.

That is v1's "monotone consistent cuts" target used as a yardstick for segmenter boundaries. v2's target adds inverted splits, which are Wu 1997's ITG binary splits (also the permutation-tree and hierarchical-alignment-tree decompositions, e.g. Stanojevic and Sima'an 2014, W14-4017). Neither line is cited.

Every other v2 part is a credited or standard component:
- the question, on FLORES with a word aligner, proposed as a tokenizer-construction criterion: 2502.06468 (re-read in full);
- the statistic: Cohen's kappa_M at a fixed budget, the chance-corrected form of segmentation agreement (Artstein and Poesio 2008, 10.1162/coli.07-034-r2; uncited);
- the consensus target: alignment intersection (Och and Ney 2003);
- the gold-path correction: Rogan-Gladen 1978;
- the random floor: a matched-count random baseline;
- line M's premise: phrasal cohesion (Fox 2002), and the converse use of alignment-derived non-constituent spans to shape monolingual structure (Kuhn 2004, 10.3115/1218955.1219015; uncited; also the lineage of Stage 1's idea);
- the object: released H-Net checkpoints.

The proposal concedes the point itself: "No method novelty is claimed ... the instrument is an assembly of credited parts". Its ledger rates the word-gap and budget rule "Medium-low (an instrument choice)" and the shared idea "High". The repair's real work shows that naive assemblies fail (S1v2: a single aligner loses about 0.3 of S; a monotone-only target reads 0.37-0.40; word-end detection is removed). That makes the instrument valid. It does not add a new idea.

The only remaining delta is applying Yarmohammadi's measurement plus ITG plus kappa_M to a new object, with both sides selected by the model and matched by bipartite matching. That pairing follows directly once each language has its own segmenter. So the header's NO_DIRECT_PRIOR_FOUND holds only under the narrow object qualifier, and "Defensible novelty delta" should not score as novelty.

This is not grounds for reject. No direct prior matches the object, and a go/no-go probe's decision value does not depend on novelty. The cap should come from the blind-discrimination and coverage rules. A fair blind packet should now include Yarmohammadi 2013, which has a stronger claim than token alignability to being the closest prior for the measurement design.
